Hi Dave,

a follow-up to my report: I have a reproducer and the root cause.

Root cause
----------

diRead() computes the inode's allocation group via BLKTOAG(), which
dereferences sbi->bmap:

    agno = BLKTOAG(agstart, JFS_SBI(ip->i_sb));   /* reads bmap->db_agl2size */

The bmap of a *live, mounted* superblock is freed and reallocated by
jfs_mount_rw(sb, remount=1) -- the path jfs_remount() takes for a
read-only -> read-write remount:

    dbUnmount(sbi->ipbmap, 1);   /* kfree(bmp);  sbi->bmap = NULL; */
    dbMount(sbi->ipbmap);        /* re-allocate (does I/O) */

A concurrent lookup (open() -> jfs_lookup() -> jfs_iget() -> diRead())
that races this window either reads the just-freed bmap (the KASAN
slab-use-after-free I reported first) or, once sbi->bmap has been
cleared, dereferences NULL.  Both faults are the same race at the same
access -- offset 52 is bmap->db_agl2size in both traces.

Reproducer
----------

The attached program repeatedly mounts the crafted image read-only and
remounts it read-write (i.e. the jfs_mount_rw(sb, 1) path) while
hammering lookups on the freshly mounted filesystem.  It triggered in
about 19 minutes on one VM and 26 minutes on another (2/2), on 7.2-rc5
with 2 vCPUs:

    [ 1142.131821] Oops: general protection fault, probably for non-canonical address 0xdffffc0000000006: 0000 [#1] SMP KASAN NOPTI
    [ 1142.132552] KASAN: null-ptr-deref in range [0x0000000000000030-0x0000000000000037]
    [ 1142.133042] CPU: 1 UID: 0 PID: 30502 Comm: jfs_race9 Tainted: G        W           7.2.0-rc5 #1 PREEMPT(full)
    [ 1142.134508] RIP: 0010:diRead+0x521/0xc40
    [ 1142.140212] Call Trace:
    [ 1142.140626]  ? __pfx_diRead+0x10/0x10
    [ 1142.140927]  jfs_iget+0x9b/0x5b0
    [ 1142.141229]  jfs_lookup+0x27a/0x340
    [ 1142.142850]  path_openat+0x293c/0x3dd0
    [ 1142.143817]  do_file_open+0x216/0x470
    [ 1142.145352]  do_sys_openat2+0xe8/0x210
    [ 1142.146270]  __x64_sys_openat+0x13f/0x1f0

Immediately before each attempt the log shows the remount transition:

    loop0: Can't mount, would change RO state

How to run it
-------------

    # in the guest, with the crafted image from my first mail:
    base64 -d jfs.zlib.b64 > jfs.zlib
    python3 -c "import zlib;open('jfs.img','wb').write(zlib.decompress(open('jfs.zlib','rb').read()))"
    losetup /dev/loop0 jfs.img           # read-write loop device
    gcc -static -O2 -pthread -o jfs_race9 jfs_race9.c
    ./jfs_race9 3600 /dev/loop0 4        # seconds, loop device, lanes

One detail that matters: jfs's rw->ro remount does not actually take
effect (the mount stays rw), so each iteration needs a *fresh* read-only
mount before the read-write remount.  That is also how syzkaller reaches
this path.

